<GuideLayout
  title="Build a content site"
  description="A headless CMS is only half the story. This Public Blog fetches a static export at build time and renders plain HTML pages."
>
  <p>
    Content clients should not import the CMS library. They talk to the Headless API — the delivery
    surface your CMS exposes to the public — and turn the response into pages, feeds, and forms.
  </p>

  <h2>Build-time export, runtime comments</h2>
  <p>The Public Blog workflow:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Example CMS runs and serves the Headless API on port 3000.</li>
    <li><code>fetch-export.ts</code> downloads a validated public export and assets.</li>
    <li>Astro builds static HTML from <code>.generated/public-export.json</code>.</li>
    <li>Comment submission is the only piece that calls the API at runtime.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Why static?</h2>
  <p>
    Posts, authors, categories, and tags change on editorial cadence — not on every page view. A
    static snapshot keeps hosting simple, makes caching trivial, and proves the Headless API is a
    clean boundary.
  </p>

  <h2>Your stack, your templates</h2>
  <p>
    This site uses Astro and Tailwind. Yours might be Next.js, Eleventy, or a mobile app. The
    contract is the export shape from the Headless API, not the library's internal types.
  </p>

  <h2>Try it locally</h2>
  <pre><code>{`# Terminal 1 — start the CMS
cd apps/example-cms && bun run dev

# Terminal 2 — build and serve the blog
cd apps/public-blog && bun run build && bun run dev`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Browse the archive, read a post, submit a comment. Then open the Example CMS dashboard to approve
    the comment and trigger a rebuild.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
